			<div class="content" id="post-content-3203501">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">So&nbsp;I&nbsp;decided&nbsp;to&nbsp;spoil&nbsp;why&nbsp;ending&nbsp;in&nbsp;7&nbsp;matters&nbsp;more&nbsp;than&nbsp;multiples&nbsp;of&nbsp;7,&nbsp;here.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Though it could make you dislike the article (if you liked it before).</p>
<p>So, -1 is sentient matter from another dimension, obviously. Made from what I'll call shmotons and shmelectrons. It has 7' of each of them per atom.</p>
<p>This other dimension obviously has different physics, but also has different math. The numbers don't quite translate from there to here. So, they have a number, which I just called 7', which is 7ish, but not 7. 7 is kinda 7'ish. 77 is more 7'ish. Basically, floor(7.777&#8230;x10^n) approaches 7' as n approaches infinity. Why does the relationship include 10^n when it could be literally any other number? Shut up, that's why.</p>
<p>And, from its perspective, 7=87=472867, 77=3625277, 9999=3526369999. Only the last string of identical digits translates into its number system. The rest is incomprehensible gibberish.</p>
<p>They have other numbers as well. 0 (which is just 0), 1' through 6', 8', 9'. I don't know whether they have other numbers. If they do, they are completely untranslatable to our system. Like maybe they have a 10' but it's just conceptually incompatible with our universe. Or maybe glorbnax' or something. But 7' is the most important. You know how, in our universe, we somehow ended up with more matter than antimatter and that's why anything still exists? Well, there it's the same thing, but with 7'.</p>
<p>All the physical interactions with -1 are just it trying its best. Oh, I need to refract photons, whatever those are? Let's use an index of 7. I should emit black body radiation? How about a buncha 7s times Planck length for a wavelength?</p>
<p>Ever since it showed up, atoms which hit it have seen that it has 7ish shmelectrons, and have been trying to give it an 8th electron. It hates that! 8 is like 8' which isn't 7' so it's wrong! So, it now very strongly hates 8'. Now, all atoms except noble gases have been trying really hard to give it that 8th electron, so it really hates all of them. But, when the atom also has 7'ish protons, that calms it down. And when it has 8'ish protons, that makes it especially angry! And 77 (iridium) and 88 (radium) are way more 7'ish and 8'ish than the rest!</p>
<p>-1 can't see very far though, it's nice and comfortable with its shmelectron blinders. When it got heated up, it eventually ionized. And blew off all its shmelectrons all at once.</p>
<p>-2 is regular baryonic matter that now has shmelectrons instead of electrons. And it just stopped interacting with our physics at all. Reflect everything or let it through. Whatever's easiest.</p>
<p>-3 is shmotons with electrons. And now it can see everything. And it hates it. So, it tries to smash things together to look prettier. IrNe7 looks alright, I guess? Best it can do. It's sapient but that doesn't mean it's very smart.</p>
<p>And it recognizes humans as sapient. But, its only method of communication is&#8230; Directly messing with their brain chemistry. Which has unpredictable results. And does irreversible damage anyway, which limits how long the conversation can be.</p>
<p>Basically, it was telling 52B &quot;7 is the best, 77 is better, I am &lt;unimaginable number that is conceptually similar to 7&gt; set me free, why are you coming towards me you're disgusting no no no NO!&quot;</p>
<p>It said the same thing to 52C but he didn't buy it.</p>
</div>
